WEBVTT

00:00:03.221 --> 00:00:07.059
The Skills card has Required skills and Skill requirements.

00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:12.078
A requirement names a Skill, a Minimum proficiency, whether it is Required, and

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.850
Relax after, in seconds.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.802
A required skill removes agents who lack it.

00:00:16.802 --> 00:00:21.526
Relax after drops the requirement once the caller has waited that long, so

00:00:21.526 --> 00:00:24.361
a rare skill cannot hold a caller forever.

00:00:24.853 --> 00:00:28.397
A requirement that is not required removes nobody.

00:00:28.397 --> 00:00:31.435
It ranks agents who have the skill higher.

00:00:31.435 --> 00:00:37.573
The agent's own skills, with a proficiency from one to five, are set on Agent entitlements.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:43.634
Agent entitlements also has Queue preferences, with a Priority and a Delay in seconds.

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:48.081
They matter when an agent becomes free and picks the next work, not

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:51.370
when a call arrives and several agents are idle.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.192
Capacity is how many interactions an agent can hold at once.

00:00:57.192 --> 00:01:01.093
It is one by default, and no screen has a field for it:

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:03.971
it comes from a recipe or a deployment.

00:01:03.971 --> 00:01:08.192
An agent on a voice call is never offered another call, so a

00:01:08.192 --> 00:01:11.070
higher capacity only matters for texts.

